<!--Post: 3083--><!--Time: 1104440778-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3083"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wilmac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wilmac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 1:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is just a theory, but what if BTK is actually more than one killer? Like, several guys got together and said &#34;Let&#39;s try to make the WPD look like idiots.&#34; So they cooked up a serial murderer, but each of the people in this little group did one murder each. That would explain why the police sketches vary so wildly. <BR> <BR>Perhaps this was a group of students in PJ Wyatt&#39;s class? <BR> <BR>That&#39;s the thing I can&#39;t get around is that each person that &#34;sees&#34; BTK and gives a discription for a sketch has such a wildly different discription from the previous one. <BR> <BR>For instance: <BR> <BR>Kevin Bright saw BTK <BR>Post Office employee saw BTK <BR>The Vian kid talked to BTK <BR> <BR>Yet, we still don&#39;t have a definitive police sketch of BTK. <BR> <BR>That just leads me to believe that either: <BR>1&#41; These people didn&#39;t actually see BTK <BR>or <BR>2&#41; BTK is more than one person <BR> <BR>Thoughts? I searched, but didn&#39;t find anyone mentioning this at length...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3083-->
<!--Post: 3115--><!--Time: 1104466085-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3115"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 8:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;m glad somebody else is bothered by this.  I agree that BTK&#39;s appearance varies significantly from one sighting to the next.  It does need an explanation.  My idea on this is that he has a very average, but malleable appearance, that people key on different aspects, and that it varies from one time to the next.  I wouldn&#39;t believe it except that I have a friend like that - every picture taken over the last decade looks like a different person.  Hair is light or dark depending upon sun exposure, straight or wavy depending on length, skin tone varying with time of year, eyes depend on lighting - everything.  I think of him as a chameleon.  He just blends in, and he never looks the same.  I look at the pictures and I swear they can&#39;t even be brothers.  I think BTK&#39;s non-striking and continuously changing appearance has proven to be one of his greatest assets in crime.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3115-->
<!--Post: 3125--><!--Time: 1104472137-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3125"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 9:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Eyewitness testimony is some of the most unreliable of all. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3125-->
<!--Post: 3136--><!--Time: 1104480484-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3136"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 31, 2004 - 12:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Excellent point, Q. IYO, could a composite be useful in this case, perhaps through some sort of averaging effect, or would it just muddy the waters further?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3136-->
<!--Post: 3158--><!--Time: 1104531936-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3158"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Justathought<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Justathought<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 31, 2004 - 2:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If BTK has a liking for disguises maybe he rubbed some sort of dark cream over his face to make him appear less white.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3158-->
<!--Post: 3168--><!--Time: 1104537842-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3168"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ryddik<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ryddik<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 31, 2004 - 4:04 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Its easy enough to change your skin color there is product used in theater productions and costumes to change whatever color you want its a powder based and put on correctly is hard to remove without the right cleaner it also looks natural by that I mean skin tones and such would look natural I&#39;ve used it many times and have been changed from light brown to bright blue.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3168-->
<!--Post: 7200--><!--Time: 1108273522-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7200"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ztargate<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ztargate<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 12, 2005 - 9:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->A BTK group makes sense to me. It would explain a few things. <BR> <BR>In the Oteros crime, how can one person, even if he&#39;s holding a gun, handle two adults and two children at once? I know for a fact that kids don&#39;t listen, specially when they are scared, yet the kids didn&#39;t scream or try to run away?  <BR> <BR>The two adults were experts in martial arts, and they knew the odds of letting the stranger tie them up. They also knew that two can take on one person holding a gun, specially if they are busy tying up one of the kids. So, why didn&#39;t they? <BR> <BR>The amount of BTK&#39;s &#34;bodily fluid&#34; at the scene also calls for more than one person. <BR> <BR>I have read the Vian case and the &#34;phone call&#34; which saved the kids has not been explained. If it was a legitimate call, then fine. But, what if one of the accomplices needed BTK right away, so BTK had to rush out, not harming the kids. He said the call &#34;saved&#34; them. Nobody answered the phone, so under which other circumstance would BTK stop what he loves best &#40;killing&#41; and rush out, if he didn&#39;t know who was on the phone? <BR> <BR>He later on writes a letter saying how bad he felt about the phone call not allowing him to do his thing. <BR> <BR>I think that a BTK group makes a lot of sense. Maybe one of them gave descriptions of the &#34;suspect&#34; to the police, but intentionally gave the wrong description. What if one was kept as a lookout outside while the other or others did their thing inside? Maybe one did the surveillance, or took turns at it, then another did the killing, maybe taking turns too. Sick, but possible. <BR> <BR>Maybe the reason they stopped killing was because they separated. It&#39;s like a gang, they can&#39;t do anything unless they are together. Maybe they reunited a few years ago and they decided to have one last curtain call by all these drops? Maybe they wanted to re-live the &#34;good ole time&#34; because they got bored. <BR> <BR>A situation like this would be hard to prove in court, unless... one of them comes clean. Maybe the DA can work a deal of immunity for one of the BTKs, and we can finally get some answers.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7200-->
<!--Post: 7237--><!--Time: 1108316534-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7237"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 13, 2005 - 9:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ztargate: Murder is what gets him off. The sexual frenzy of each kill causes a renewed arousal. He very well could have produced that all by his lonesome. Years ago, a man on parole in my town kidnapped 2 women. He sexually assaulted one, then masturbated. He sexually assaulted the other and then masturbated again. He kept this up all night. Hopefully, he went blind afterward. It is possible. I think that BTK is in a state similar to someone on a drug high, in which the person demonstates super-human strength. In my police academy, I saw the reenactment of a crime event in which two officers confronted an older man burglarizing a condo. Within seconds, the perp killed the rookie female officer. The other officer shot the man almost point blank in the back of his head. It did not phase him. The officer managed to knock the man down, counted his ribs, and again point-blank, shot thru the man&#39;s lungs and heart. The perp said &#39;Oh, you got me a good one&#39; and stood up. The man attacked the officer and the officer emptied his gun into the floor as the perp was about to get his gun away from him. He managed to reload and bring the man down. The older man was high on drugs of some kind. It is the same thing when someone can stand up with 800 lbs. of police officers sitting on him, as happened in California several years ago. How can one manage to lift a car or heavy object at the scene of an accident? BTK is not normal and is even less human when he is killing. I think that he acts alone because he cannot trust anyone with his secret and his life, he would be intellectually superior to most, and he wants the glory all to himself.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7237-->
<!--Post: 7238--><!--Time: 1108317976-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7238"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 13, 2005 - 10:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The easiest way to hold two adults and two children at gunpoint is to hold the gun to the head of the smallest child and tell the oldest child to tie the parents up up. then have the smallest child tie the oldest child up. then check the knots. then tie the youngest child up. <BR> <BR>then move each to their respective positions using the same basic theory.  <BR> <BR>There is no way, even if the parents know they will all be killed, that a parent is going to make the move that causes the death of their child.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7238-->
<!--Post: 7265--><!--Time: 1108338515-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7265"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Suspiria_2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Suspiria_2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 13, 2005 - 3:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->i theorize, without any basis in fact at all, that BTK made the mother tie the children up first, perhaps in a side bedroom.  i think she would have done this and cooperated if he led her to believe that rape or theft was his object.  he then takes her off to her own room and ties her up himself, after a violent struggle.   <BR> <BR>this probably seems backwards to most of you, as it would make sense for Mrs. Otero to fight first and then be subdued through threats, but that is just not what my instinct is telling me.  i believe btk would have had the full cooperation of the mother as long as the children were present and there was a hope of preserving their lives.  it seems that she would fight only as the realization dawned on her that, once she was tied up, they would all most likely die. <BR> <BR>Mr. Otero then arrives home and he threatens him with a gun, the knowledge that he already has everyone else in the home subdued, and perhaps the guarantee that theft is his only object.  this is also based on instinct because i seriously doubt that a military man like Mr. Otero would go down without a fight for any reason at all unless it was to preserve his family. <BR> <BR>he would not take the risk of allowing the two adults to tie each other.  both of them know martial arts, and if they are at any time both free at once, i see them acting in concert to wrest control away from btk.  it also seems unlikely that he would make the children tie their mother up first, simply because they would not have the knowledge or inclination to do it properly.  he would definitely not tie her up himself with two scared children loose, as that situation is just too unpredictable.  even with an injunction from the mother to &#34;do whatever he says&#34; i think it is likely that a child will start to scream, run, or whatever other instinctual actions come into play when self-preservation is at stake. <BR> <BR>sorry to take so long, and this theory is probably out in various posts on this board.  it just seems to make sense to me to parlay his control over the most vulnerable victims into control over the more dangerous ones.  especially if he wanted to have the option of later taking the time with each victim necessary for his gratification.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7265-->
<!--Post: 7266--><!--Time: 1108338934-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7266"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Suspiria_2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Suspiria_2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 13, 2005 - 3:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->oops!  sorry about the off topic post above. <BR> <BR>my only defense is to claim that a conspiracy is unlikely.  the whole &#34;two can keep a secret if one is dead&#34; thing arises. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7266-->
<!--Post: 7468--><!--Time: 1108508799-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7468"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ztargate<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ztargate<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 15, 2005 - 3:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It has happened before, that there have been &#34;teams&#34; acting as a serial killer.  <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.serial-killer.org" target=_top>http://www.serial-killer.org</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
